\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The groupoid of enriched four-cycle backgrounds}
\date{2026-10-18}

\p{A classification of Gaussian backgrounds must retain the maps that
transport their observables. Consider the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003)
with vertices #{V=\mathbb Z/4\mathbb Z}, forward edges #{e_i:i\to i+1},
and weights #{(a,b,a,b)}, where #{a,b>0}. Choose #{m>0} and one of
#{R_0=\{0,2\}}, #{R_1=\{1,3\}} as retained vertices. Write
#{x=(a,b,m,R_j)} and let #{X} be the set of all these tuples.
The tuple specifies a [finite Gaussian background](fdip-000G), together
with its named [half-turn](fdip-000J).}

\p{More explicitly, retain the carrier, counting reference pairing,
weight matrix #{W_x=\operatorname{diag}(a,b,a,b)}, and
##{L_x=B^TW_xB,\qquad Q_x=L_x+m^2I.}
Here #{(B\phi)_i=\phi_{i+1}-\phi_i}. For #{\phi\ne0},
##{\begin{aligned}
\phi^TQ_x\phi&=\sum_iw_i(\phi_{i+1}-\phi_i)^2\\
 &\quad+m^2\|\phi\|^2>0.
\end{aligned}}
The [normalized Gaussian](fdip-0005) #{\mu_{Q_x}} is determined
by this precision and coordinate Lebesgue measure. Also retain the
coordinate projection #{\pi_{R_j}:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^{R_j}}
and the named action #{\rho(h)\delta_i=\delta_{i+2}} of
#{C_2=\{1,h\}}. The vertex basis #{\delta_i(v)=\mathbf1_{v=i}}
is distinct from the edges #{e_i} and the [spectral vectors](fdip-000B)
#{u_a}. These structures are specified or determined, rather than
additional freely variable coordinates of #{X}.}

\p{Allow rotations and reflections of the underlying cycle. Put
##{D_4=\langle r,s\mid r^4=s^2=1,\ srs=r^{-1}\rangle,}
where #{r(i)=i+1} and #{s(i)=-i}. A reflection reverses the forward
orientation. Thus these arrows use the [signed-edge convention](fdip-000J)
and extend the earlier strictly directed [exact relabelings](fdip-000G).
For #{g=r^ks^\varepsilon}, with #{k\in\mathbb Z/4} and
#{\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}}, set
##{\begin{aligned}
g(i)&=k+(-1)^\varepsilon i,\\
P_g\delta_i&=\delta_{g(i)},\\
\tau_g(i)&=\begin{cases}k+i&\varepsilon=0,\\k-i-1&\varepsilon=1,\end{cases}\\
U_g\eta_i&=(-1)^\varepsilon\eta_{\tau_g(i)}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{\eta_i} is the edge-field coordinate basis. All indices are
modulo four. The eight vertex maps are distinct because their values
at #{0,1} determine #{k,\varepsilon}.}

\p{The edge indexed by #{\tau_g(i)} has endpoints #{g(i),g(i+1)},
in forward order for a rotation and reverse order for a reflection.
Consequently, for every field #{\phi},
##{\begin{aligned}
(BP_g\phi)_{\tau_g(i)}&=(-1)^\varepsilon(B\phi)_i,\\
BP_g&=U_gB.
\end{aligned}}
If #{k'} denotes a second group element, endpoint transport and
multiplication of the orientation signs give
##{P_{k'g}=P_{k'}P_g,\qquad U_{k'g}=U_{k'}U_g.}
For an explicit group-law check, composition of
#{(k,\varepsilon)} followed by #{(\ell,\eta)} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
(\ell,\eta)(k,\varepsilon)
 &=(\ell+(-1)^\eta k,\eta+\varepsilon).
\end{aligned}}
The second coordinate is taken modulo two.
Both coordinate maps are orthogonal, and their inverses are indexed
by #{g^{-1}}.}

\p{Transport weights by #{W_{gx}=U_gW_xU_g^T} and the retained set
by #{R\mapsto gR}. The signs cancel in the diagonal weight matrix.
The weights swap exactly when #{k+\varepsilon} is odd, while
#{R_j\mapsto R_{j+k\bmod2}}. In particular,
##{\begin{aligned}
r(a,b,m,R_j)&=(b,a,m,R_{j+1}),\\
s(a,b,m,R_j)&=(b,a,m,R_j).
\end{aligned}}
The family is closed under these maps, with mass unchanged.
Using #{BP_g^T=U_g^TB} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
L_{gx}&=B^TU_gW_xU_g^TB=P_gL_xP_g^T,\\
Q_{gx}&=P_gQ_xP_g^T.
\end{aligned}}
Moreover #{g(i+2)=g(i)+2}, so every arrow commutes with the
same named half-turn: #{P_g\rho(h)=\rho(h)P_g}.}

\p{Form the [action groupoid](fgap-000P) #{D_4\ltimes X} by setting
##{\operatorname{Hom}(x,y)=\{g\in D_4:g x=y\}.}
The group element remains part of an arrow. Compose #{g:x\to y}
and #{k:y\to z} as #{kg:x\to z}; the action law gives its target.
The identity is #{1:x\to x}, the inverse is
#{g^{-1}:gx\to x}, and the two inverse identities and associativity
follow from the group laws. Thus every arrow is invertible, as in
\citet{Definition 4.2.5, tag 0018}{stacks2017stacks}.
The source, target and composition follow the set-level pattern in
\citet{Lemma 78.15.1, tag 0444}{stacks2017stacks}; that lemma is
stated for algebraic spaces, whereas the axioms here were checked
directly for sets. #{X} is infinite, but each orbit and its restricted
groupoid is finite.}

\p{The [stabilizer](frap-000Y) of the weights at #{a\ne b} is
#{\{1,r^2,rs,r^3s\}\cong C_2\times C_2}; preserving weights
requires #{k+\varepsilon} even. The stabilizer of either retained
partition is #{\{1,r^2,s,r^2s\}\cong C_2\times C_2}, since
preserving the partition requires #{k} even. Intersecting these
conditions gives the following automorphism groups.
When #{a\ne b},
##{\operatorname{Aut}(x)=\{1,r^2\}\cong C_2.}
When #{a=b},
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Aut}(x)&=\{1,r^2,s,r^2s\}\\
 &\cong C_2\times C_2.
\end{aligned}}
At equal weights the first condition disappears. In each four-element
subgroup the two indicated generators are distinct commuting
involutions. No further condition comes from the named half-turn,
which commutes with all eight elements. The kernel #{\{1,r^2\}}
of the parameter action is retained as distinct arrows:
#{P_{r^2}\delta_0=\delta_2\ne\delta_0}.}

\p{For unequal weights the orbit consists of both weight orders and
both partitions, at fixed mass. Write
#{x_j=(a,b,m,R_j)} and #{y_j=(b,a,m,R_j)}. The drawing shows
selected generator arrows; it does not draw all arrows or isotropy loops.}

\tikzfigs{0.72}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (x0) at (0,0) {$x_0$};
\node (y0) at (4,0) {$y_0$};
\node (y1) at (0,-2.6) {$y_1$};
\node (x1) at (4,-2.6) {$x_1$};
\draw[arrow] (x0) -- node[above=3mm] {$s$} (y0);
\draw[arrow] (y1) -- node[below=3mm] {$s$} (x1);
\draw[arrow] (x0) -- node[left=3mm] {$r$} (y1);
\draw[arrow] (y0) -- node[right=3mm] {$r$} (x1);
\node at (2,-1.3) {$\operatorname{Aut}=C_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{At #{a=b}, put #{z_j=(a,a,m,R_j)}. There are two objects,
each with automorphism group #{C_2\times C_2}:}

\tikzfigs{0.72}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (z0) at (0,0) {$z_0$};
\node (z1) at (4,0) {$z_1$};
\draw[arrow] (z0) -- node[above=3mm] {$r$} (z1);
\node at (2,-1.1) {$\operatorname{Aut}=C_2\times C_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The generators #{s,r} reach every displayed object; there are
exactly four objects when #{a\ne b} and two when #{a=b}.
It follows in both directions that the coarse isomorphism classes are
#{(m,\{a,b\})}. The coarse orbit set forgets the isotropy just
computed, as in \ref{fgap-000P}. On the strict slice
#{(1,t,m,R_0)}, isomorphism requires the same mass and
#{\{1,t\}=\{1,t'\}}, whose products give #{t=t'}.
Thus reciprocal parameters are not identified there unless #{t=1}.
This is a set-level groupoid, within the [boundary before stacks](fgap-000U).}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{CategoryTheory.ActionCategory} constructs
the generic action category; a group action makes it a groupoid.
\leanref{CategoryTheory.ActionCategory.stabilizerIsoEnd}
identifies the stabilizer monoid with endomorphisms, which are invertible
in the group case. \leanref{MulAction.orbitRel} and
\leanref{MulAction.stabilizer} provide the generic coarse relation
and subgroup. \leanref{DihedralGroup.card} gives eight elements
at #{n=4}, and \leanref{DihedralGroup.center_eq_closure_of_even_ne_two}
gives the central half-turn. In its constructor convention our
#{r^ks} corresponds to #{\texttt{sr}(-k)}, while #{r^k}
corresponds to #{\texttt{r}(k)}. These declarations support the
group constructions; the particular parameter action, signed incidence
and stabilizer intersections are the calculations above.}
